  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\u27f6[\\begin{aligned}-8x+4y \\\\ 6x-3y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$",
  "worked_solution": "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot 3+4\\cdot k \\\\ 6\\cdot 3-3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot 3-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-24+4k \\\\ 18-3k \\\\ 6-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-24+4k=0 \\\\ 18-3k=0 \\\\ 6-k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=6.$",
